|> Exercise
    handle=_project_i_and_j_

    What are the orthogonal (= perpendicular) 
    projections of the
    vectors $(1, 0)$, $(0, 1)$ 
    onto a $(-1, 1)$-ruler
    placed at angle $\theta$ in the unit circle?

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_statement.svg

    (I.e., find the ruler-readings
    marked ‘1’ and ‘2’ above
    as a function of the angle $\theta$.)

    |> Solution

        _Solution 1._ If we rotate the diagram by $-\theta$
        to align the ruler with the $x$ axis the new angles
        of the vectors are $-\theta$ and $\eta - \theta$
        (changed from $0$ and $\eta$, respectively):
        
        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol1.svg

        The ruler-readings are therefore
        $$
        \cos(-\theta) = \cos(\theta)
        $$
        and
        $$
        \cos(\eta - \theta) = \sin(\theta)
        $$

        respectively (in order ‘1’, ‘2’), making use of
        basic identities. (See the section “Identities with $\eta$”
        in this chapter.)

        |> Pause

        _Solution 2._ 
        Recalling first...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ch_unit_circle_with_new_definition.svg

        ...this figure from the chapter, we note that
        there is an alternate
        set of definitions for sin and cos that is 
        based on clockwise instead of counterclockwise rotation:
        
        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol2.svg

        Now comparing this set of definitions to
        this figure...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol3.svg

        ...in which, by contrast to usual, angles are measured 

        __positively clockwise__

        (that's why it's “$\theta$” not “$-\theta$”) 
        it becomes trivial to see that the two ruler-readings
        are just $\cos(\theta)$ (‘1’) and $\sin(\theta)$ (‘2’).

        |> Pause

        _Solution 3._ 
        More generally, when a unit
        vector is projected onto a “unit ruler”,
        we can switch the ruler _&_ vector positions
        without affecting the ruler-reading:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol4.svg

        (Because what counts is the angle between the
        “forward end” of the ruler and the forward end of
        the vector, measured as an angle between $0^\circ$
        and $180^\circ$, and this angle remains the same.)
        If we apply this to the problem statement
        the unique ruler is replaced by a unique vector
        while the two vectors are each replaced by a ruler:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_sol5_v2.svg

        ...and in the new configuration, at bottom right, 
        one can see by the original definitions of sin
        and cos that the projections are
        are $\cos(\theta)$ (‘1’), $\sin(\theta)$ (‘2’).

        |> SolutionNote

            !! The original definition of cosine is as pictured
            !! in the next graphic, with a rotating point that is
            !! projected onto a fixed axis, namely the $x$ axis:
            !! While the original definition of cosine rotates
            !! a point and then projects that point onto the $x$ axis...
            !! take the $x$-coordinate of a rotating point:

            !! While the original definitions of sin and cos
            !! involve a point rotating around the unit circle,
            !! projected in two different directions, as in the
            !! following diagrams...

            The original definitions of sin and cos involve
            a single point rotating around the unit circle projected
            onto two fixed rulers, resulting in two
            functions:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_note1.svg

            |> Pause

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_note1_sin_v2.svg

            !! But this exercise shows that the definition can be
            !! turned on its head, so that cosine is obtained by
            !! keeping the point fixed while rotating the ruler, instead:
            !! projecting a fixed point onto a rotating axis, instead:
            !! ...this exercise shows that we can also characterize
            !! sin and cos as the projections of two separate fixed points
            !! onto a single rotating ruler, essentially turning the 
            !! entire definition on its head:
            By contrast, this exercise shows that we can also view
            sin and cos as the projections of two different fixed points onto
            a single ruler, which is the rotating object:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_note2.svg

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_project_i_and_j_note2_sin.svg

            To paraphrase, according to the first definition,
            sin and cos are

            __the measurements obtained by virtue of being
            at a certain position (angle) in the plane__

            while according to the second definition, sin and cos
            are 

            __the measurements obtained by virtue of
            measuring according to a certain direction in the plane__

            where in the former case, to underscore the philosophical difference,
            it is the thing being
            measured that varies, while in the latter case,
            it is the instrument of measurement that varies.
            !! which is kind of cool. (?)
            
            !! which underscores a kind of “duality” between the
            !! concept of an object being measured, and the tool
            !! that is measuring it—by fixing the object to be
            !! constant, and letting the tool range over all possible
            !! measuring tools, the tables have turned, with the object
            !! suddenly becoming a measurement device of the tools! 
            !! (I.e.,
            !! because the object is ascribing a number to each of the
            !! measuring tools—its own measure!) 
            !! So to paraphrase again, the traditional definitions
            !! of sin and cos follow the mold of

            !! __points as measured by a specific ruler__

            !! (for each of sin and cos individually, we mean),
            !! while the
            !! newly found characterizations of sin and cos follow the
            !! mold of

            !! __rulers as measured by a specific point__

            !! if we lean in to the metaphor of a measuring device
            !! being “measured” by the thing it is supposed to measure.

